# Yonodex Tor Configuration
# Whitepaper Layer 4, Section 6.1: Tor Onion Transport
#
# This torrc is bundled with the Yonodex client and launched automatically
# on app startup. It runs a minimal Tor daemon that:
#   1. Exposes a SOCKS5 proxy on localhost:9050 for libp2p transport
#   2. Hosts a v3 .onion hidden service pointing at the local libp2p listener
#   3. Uses restricted permissions and no external control port

# ---- Data & logs ----
DataDirectory data\tor-data
GeoIPFile data\geoip
GeoIPv6File data\geoip6
Log notice file data\tor.log

# ---- SOCKS proxy for libp2p ----
# libp2p will route peer connections through this SOCKS5 port.
# Bound to localhost only - never exposed externally.
SocksPort 9050
SocksPolicy accept 127.0.0.1
SocksPolicy reject *

# ---- Hidden service (our .onion identity) ----
# Tor generates an Ed25519 keypair on first launch and derives the .onion
# address from it. The key stays in this directory forever, giving the node
# a stable identity across restarts.
HiddenServiceDir data\onion-service
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 4001 127.0.0.1:4001
HiddenServiceMaxStreams 25

# ---- Circuit hardening (whitepaper Section 6.1) ----
# 3-hop circuits by default; guard rotation limited to prevent profiling.
CircuitBuildTimeout 60
LearnCircuitBuildTimeout 1
MaxCircuitDirtiness 600
NewCircuitPeriod 30

# ---- Disable unused features ----
# No exit traffic - we only run as a client and hidden service.
ExitPolicy reject *:*
# No control port - the app manages tor via process lifecycle, not control protocol.
ControlPort 0

# ---- Client behavior ----
# Only bootstrap from directory authorities and known relays.
ClientOnly 1
# Do not fetch microdescriptors for all relays - keep it lean.
FetchDirInfoEarly 1
FetchDirInfoExtraEarly 1